                <pb facs="1015" n="995" sameAs="frus:frus1950v07/pg995"/>

                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Editorial Note</hi>
                </head>
                <p>On October 24, General MacArthur issued orders to his ground commanders in Korea
                    removing all restrictions on the use of United Nations forces in North Korea and
                    instructing them to proceed, using all their forces and not just Republic of
                    Korea troops, to the northern frontiers of North Korea. The Joint Chiefs of
                    Staff questioned these orders as not being in accord with the directive of
                    September 27, to which General MacArthur replied on the following day that the
                    lifting of restrictions was demanded by military necessity, since Republic of
                    Korea troops could not handle the situation in northern North Korea alone.
                    General MacArthur added that he felt he had latitude <pb facs="1016" n="996"
                        xml:id="pg996"/> to alter his directive on the basis of Secretary Marshall’s
                    telegram to him on September 29, <ref target="#pg826">page 826</ref>. (See
                    Appleman, <hi rendition="#italic">South to the Naktang. North to the Yalu</hi>,
                    pages 670–671; <hi rendition="#italic">Hearings</hi>, pages 1240–1241.)</p>
            </div>
